<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warsaw Pact</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Warsaw Treaty Organization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> Pact was the military alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the communist states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, signed in Warsaw on 14 May 1955 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>. Its forces were used against members, as in the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1968. Albania withdrew in 1968, and the pact was dissolved on 1 July 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact.jpg" alt="Warsaw Pact" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob984 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warsaw_Pact_in_1990_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1948 the Czechoslovak People's Army became a pillar of the <b>Warsaw Pact</b>, and Czechoslovak arms went to Israel in 1948 and Egypt in 1955.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Refusing the <b>Warsaw Pact</b> and NATO alike, it became a founding member of the Non-Aligned Movement with India, Egypt and Indonesia; Tito made 169 official visits to 92 nations and carried the Algerian FLN's demands to the United Nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Soviet Union then defeated Japan in Manchuria in August 1945, tested an atomic bomb in 1949, formed the <b>Warsaw Pact</b> in 1955 and built a vast nuclear arsenal and ocean-going navy, reaching rough strategic parity with the United States by the early 1970s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…rapid bi-polarisation of the world in the Cold War, when two great powers formed blocs, the pro-Soviet socialist bloc of the <b>Warsaw Pact</b> and the pro-American capitalist group, many of whose members belonged to NATO, and set out to pull the rest of the world into their orbits.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Solidarity is a Polish trade union founded at the Gdańsk Shipyard in August 1980, the first independent union recognised in a <b>Warsaw Pact</b> country.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>His reforms, 'socialism with a human face', inspired the Prague Spring, ended by a <b>Warsaw Pact</b> invasion in August.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
